``` GhostFrame: ESXi Hypervisor Implant Linked to Settra Ransomware (TAG-218)
    Field names follow CIM/UDM conventions and have not been tested against live data; adjust them to your environment.
    Splunk hunt over raw events, for data not mapped to CIM. Author: daniyyell-dev. https://github.com/daniyyell-dev/rapid-threat-intel/tree/main/threats/2026-11_November-2026/2026-11-11/GhostFrame-ESXi-Hypervisor-Implant-Settra-Ransomware
    Sources: https://www.recordedfuture.com/blog/ghostframe-esxi-implant-settra-ransomware
    Define the rti_network, rti_dns, rti_web and rti_endpoint macros once (example: all/splunk_macros.conf).
    Each macro takes one argument, a list of search terms used to pre-filter the raw events, and must only use
    streaming commands (search, eval, rename, rex, fields, spath) so it can run inside multisearch.
    Set the time picker to at least the last 30 days. The indicators below are live and malicious.
    Output: first_seen, last_seen, ioc, ioc_type, data_model, host, user, src, dest, detail, count

    Behaviours (indicators of attack) are extra sections below, with ioc_type "behaviour (alert)" or "behaviour (hunt)".
    Behaviours are leads, not confirmed indicators, and can match legitimate activity. Compare any hit against your own baseline and environment before acting.
    Behaviours are for hunting and alerting only; never block on them.
    Sections that read raw logs use index=*: replace it with your own index names to make them faster. ```
| multisearch
  [ search `rti_network(45.158.196.81 OR 45.158.196.141 OR 193.5.65.114)`
    | search dest_ip IN ("45.158.196.81", "45.158.196.141", "193.5.65.114")
    | eval data_model="network", ioc_type="ipv4", ioc=dest_ip, src=src_ip, dest=dest_ip, detail="connection to IOC, port ".dest_port.", ".action
  ]
  [ search `rti_network(45.158.196.81 OR 45.158.196.141 OR 193.5.65.114)`
    | search src_ip IN ("45.158.196.81", "45.158.196.141", "193.5.65.114")
    | eval data_model="network", ioc_type="ipv4", ioc=src_ip, src=src_ip, dest=dest_ip, detail="connection from IOC, port ".dest_port.", ".action
  ]
  [ search `rti_endpoint(a05eae6d07b82e2e5451a72266f21fbaf76486ef4920a820988de93e15353a10)`
    | search file_hash IN ("*a05eae6d07b82e2e5451a72266f21fbaf76486ef4920a820988de93e15353a10*") OR process_hash IN ("*a05eae6d07b82e2e5451a72266f21fbaf76486ef4920a820988de93e15353a10*") OR parent_process_hash IN ("*a05eae6d07b82e2e5451a72266f21fbaf76486ef4920a820988de93e15353a10*")
    | eval matched=file_hash." ".process_hash." ".parent_process_hash
    | rex field=matched "(?i)(?<ioc>a05eae6d07b82e2e5451a72266f21fbaf76486ef4920a820988de93e15353a10)"
    | eval data_model="endpoint", ioc_type=case(len(ioc)==64, "sha256", len(ioc)==40, "sha1", 1==1, "md5"), host=dest, detail=coalesce(process, file_path)
  ]
| fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
| stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY ioc ioc_type data_model host user src dest detail

``` Behaviour 1 (Alert): One host trying six C2 ports at once
    Look for: One host opening near-simultaneous TCP connections to the same external IP on ports 53, 2222, 3389, 4444, 8443 and 9443, retried every 5 to 12 seconds
    Log source: Firewall, NetFlow or EDR network events from ESXi management addresses
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    search `rti_network(53 OR 2222 OR 3389 OR 4444 OR 8443 OR 9443)`
    | search dest_port IN (53, 2222, 3389, 4444, 8443, 9443)
    | bin _time span=30s
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen dc(dest_port) AS ports values(dest_port) AS port_list BY src_ip dest_ip _time
    | where ports>=4
    | stats sum(count) AS count min(first_seen) AS first_seen max(last_seen) AS last_seen values(port_list) AS port_list BY src_ip dest_ip
    | eval host=src_ip, user="-", src=src_ip, dest=dest_ip, detail="ports ".mvjoin(port_list, ",")." to one address within 30s", data_model="network"
    | fields - src_ip dest_ip port_list
    | eval ioc="One host trying six C2 ports at once", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]

``` Behaviour 2 (Alert): GhostFrame persistence on ESXi
    Look for: Changes to /etc/rc.local.d/local.sh, a file at /opt/vmhostd-core, or a process named vmhostd (not vmware-hostd)
    Log source: ESXi shell.log and syslog, host file checks
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    search index=* ("vmhostd-core" OR "rc.local.d/local.sh" OR "auto-backup.sh")
    | eval host=host, user=coalesce(user, "-"), src=coalesce(src, src_ip, "-"), dest=sourcetype, detail=substr(replace(_raw, "\s+", " "), 1, 300)
    | fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY host user src dest detail
    | eval data_model="ESXi logs"
    | eval ioc="GhostFrame persistence on ESXi", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]
| append [
    search `rti_endpoint(vmhostd)`
    | where match(process_name, "(?i)(^|[\\/])(vmhostd)$")
    | eval host=dest, user=user, src="-", dest=dest, detail=coalesce(parent_process_name, "-")." > ".coalesce(process, process_name, "-")
    | fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY host user src dest detail
    | eval data_model="endpoint"
    | eval ioc="GhostFrame persistence on ESXi", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]

``` Behaviour 3 (Alert): ESXi firewall disabled again and again
    Look for: The ESXi firewall being disabled (esxcli network firewall set --enabled false) or execInstalledOnly set to 0, again and again
    Log source: ESXi shell.log, hostd.log and syslog
    Lead, not proof: compare any hit with your own baseline before acting. ```
| append [
    search index=* (("firewall" "--enabled false") OR ("execInstalledOnly" 0))
    | eval host=host, user=coalesce(user, "-"), src=coalesce(src, src_ip, "-"), dest=sourcetype, detail=substr(replace(_raw, "\s+", " "), 1, 300)
    | fillnull value="-" host user src dest detail
    | stats count min(_time) AS first_seen max(_time) AS last_seen BY host user src dest detail
    | eval data_model="ESXi logs"
    | eval ioc="ESXi firewall disabled again and again", ioc_type="behaviour (alert)"
]
| sort 0 - last_seen
| eval first_seen=strftime(first_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S"), last_seen=strftime(last_seen, "%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S")
| table first_seen last_seen ioc ioc_type data_model host user src dest detail count

``` Behaviours to check by hand (no reliable Splunk field mapping, so no search is written):

    Behaviour 4 (Hunt): VNC, snapshot and tunnel activity on ESXi
        Look for: VNC enabled on VMs, unexpected snapshots or power changes, and the ESXi Host Client reached through an unusual tunnel
        Log source: ESXi and vCenter events
        Why no search: vCenter and ESXi event names for VNC settings, snapshots and power changes vary by version; review them against your change records

    Behaviours are leads, not confirmed indicators, and can match legitimate activity. Compare any hit against your own baseline and environment before acting. Behaviours are for hunting and alerting only; never block on them. ```
``` Behavioural hunt notes:
    On ESXi, a modified /etc/rc.local.d/local.sh that runs esxcli network firewall set --enabled false or sets execInstalledOnly to 0 is GhostFrame persistence.
    Settra ransomware intrusions used MeshAgent for persistence; an unexpected MeshAgent install on a server is worth checking. ```
